# Developing TraceBi

You are changing the TraceBi framework, not building a report with it.

- **`CLAUDE.md` is your rulebook.** Read it in full before you change code: the
  invariants, anti-patterns, the discoverability definition of done, and the
  commands.
- **`AGENTS.md` is for agents that use TraceBi** to build reports in a
  project. Follow it only when your change touches the authoring surface
  (the `data-tb-*` grammar, `report.json`, the MCP tools). Then update
  `tracebi/capabilities.py`, `AGENTS.md` and `tracebi/_scaffold/init_agents.md`
  in the same change; `tests/test_agent_guides.py` enforces it.
- **The live plan is `docs/strategy/epics.md`.** GitHub issues labeled
  `agent-ready` are the work. Do only what your issue asks.

## Working an issue

1. One issue, one branch (`agent/<issue-number>-<short-name>`), one draft pull
   request. Never push to `main`, never merge, never force-push someone
   else's branch.
2. Run `pytest tests/` before you change anything, so you know the starting
   state, and again before you push. Run `ruff check .` too.
3. Write the test for the issue's "done when" first when you can, then make
   it pass.
4. Add a CHANGELOG entry under `[Unreleased]` for anything a user would
   notice.
5. Fill in the pull request template. Link the issue with `Closes #<n>`.
6. If the issue needs a decision it doesn't make, stop and comment on the
   issue with the question and your proposal. Don't guess.
7. Don't let tests leave files in the repo's `output/` directory.
8. When a review on your pull request asks for changes, make exactly those
   changes on the same branch, push, and reply on the pull request with what
   you changed, item by item. Don't widen the change. If you disagree with an
   item, say why in the reply instead of skipping it silently.
